+- **The universal carve** is `a11y-overrides.css`. From 11.1.1, under
+  `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, a rule in its own last layer (`reduced-motion`) sets
+  every element's `transition-duration` to 0 and snaps its animations to their end, and it
+  mints no `transition-property`. It loses to anything unlayered or `!important`, so a
+  site's own fade keeps its clock, and a glass fade that must survive is a carve: an
+  `!important` declaration in `components` (the reveal, the Toast's dissolve, the Vue fade
+  classes). `[data-allow-motion]` is not carved here: a user who asks for reduced motion
+  outranks a per-element allowance (accessibility is absolute). The attribute does one
+  other job: it keeps an element's own transition through the theme flip's
+  `html.no-transition`. The `DarkModeToggle`'s glyph does not move under reduced motion:
+  its change rides the theme's one crossfade, an opacity change on the compositor of
+  150 ms (O-428; O-353's ruling on the theme flip).
